  "articleBody": "Custom built-ins are permanent home improvements. When planning shelving, avoiding structural and layout oversights protects your walls and keeps your storage sturdy.\n\n1. Designing Before Checking Wall Conditions: Building cases without checking for bowed studs or unlevel floors leads to gaps and crooked shelving.\n2. Fastening Only to Hollow Drywall: Anchoring heavy units with drywall anchors invites collapse under load. Fasten structural ledgers directly into verified wall framing.\n3. Ignoring Existing Trim Projections: Failing to account for thick baseboards or crown molding creates awkward offsets. Plan whether to remove, notch, or wrap existing trim.\n4. Drilling Without Verifying Hidden Utilities: Fastening into walls without checking for concealed wiring, plumbing pipes, or ductwork risks severe property damage.\n5. Underestimating Shelf Span and Load: Using thin materials across wide spans causes noticeable shelf sag once loaded with books or equipment.\n6. Omitting Structural Leveling and Shims: Resting built-in bases directly on an uneven floor transfers twists into the face frames and doors.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid\nA successful shelving project always accounts for structural anchoring and load limits before the first board is cut.\n\nNeed sturdy built-in shelving built right for your home? Contact HH Remodeling LLC at 303-505-0866 to get started with an on-site consultation.",
